{"title":"2024 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Clever_Manipulation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Clever Manipulation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Descartes’s_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Descartes’s Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Advanced_Guesswork)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Advanced Guesswork)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Essentially_Solution_3)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Essentially Solution 3)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Metasolving)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Metasolving)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_7(practically_similar_to_the_other_solutions)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7(practically similar to the other solutions)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(In_Less_Than_5_Mins_⚡🚀)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (In Less Than 5 Mins ⚡🚀)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two straight pipes (circular cylinders), with radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, lie parallel and in contact on a flat floor. The figure below shows a head-on view. What is the sum of the possible radii of a third parallel pipe lying on the same floor and in contact with both?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/9/4091eff69445abb83b260b3ccbdfa4e00b8f9182.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(6cm); draw(circle((0,1),1), linewidth(1.2)); draw((-1,0)--(1.25,0), linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((1,1/4),1/4), linewidth(1.2)); [/asy]\" width=\"285\" height=\"255\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2c42e36e7c9973e78340c793b24850e701012f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}~\\frac{1}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}~1 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}~\\frac{10}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}~\\frac{11}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac{19}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"394\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that the sum of radii of two circles tangent to each other will equal to the distance from center to center. Set the center of the big circle be at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/d/6bd0ca22e23123ae7f5c26e169721a547d26cca7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> Since both circles are tangent to a line (in this case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />), the y-coordinates of the centers are just its radius. \n</p><p>Hence, the center of the smaller circle is at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad852163ceb6b4fc891598b937fda1baef5748d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(x_2, \\frac14\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"67\" height=\"45\" />. From the the sum of radii and distance formula, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/5/6a5713f88ee4af43b6cc450f6d136fb0ec270203.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1+\\frac14 = \\sqrt{x_2^2 + \\left(\\frac34\\right)^2} \\Rightarrow x_2 = 1.\\]\" width=\"254\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>So, the coordinates of the center of the smaller circle are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/6246cc20ea4457763bc627605266ae49a472ddcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, \\frac14).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"51\" height=\"38\" /> Now, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/9/a59d48e6bcbdbd5d538b345c111d11c26deb536d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x_3,r_3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" /> be the coordinates of the new circle. Then, from the fact that sum of radii of this circle and the circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is equal to the distance from the two centers, you have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be5abef49d028b99294f2f599976a04f97d9b85c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{(x_3-0)^2+(r_3-1)^2} = 1+r_3.\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"32\" />\nSimilarly, from the fact that the sum of radii of this circle and the circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/d/4cd0f9f9869b39e79527f9010293adf7bfe5210f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, you have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/3/e63eabd90f21bcedc1d6af0dd57258e765894c66.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{(x_3-1)^2+\\left(r_3-\\frac14\\right)^2}= \\frac14 + r_3.\\]\" width=\"272\" height=\"53\" />\nSquaring the first equation, you have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf19f703d24dfa1c9c588e887a92e283f7cc1a7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_3^2+r_3^2-2r_3+1=1+2r_3+r_3^2 \\Rightarrow 4r_3=x_3^2 \\Rightarrow x_3=2\\sqrt{r_3}.\\]\" width=\"461\" height=\"20\" />\nSquaring the second equation, you have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/1/6219d62ac937b42fe3fefb7716c5e708cbc9f8c6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_3^2-2x_3+1+r_3^2-\\frac{r_3}{2}+\\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{r_3}{2}+r_3^2 \\Rightarrow x_3^2-2x_3+1=r_3\\]\" width=\"514\" height=\"37\" />\nPlugging in from the first equation we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13ccda561a3fc20f49dbca966b56f8f8d1cc552.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_3-1=x_3^2-2x_3=4r_3-4\\sqrt{r_3} \\Rightarrow 3r_3-4\\sqrt{r_3}+1=0 \\Rightarrow (3\\sqrt{r_3}-1)(\\sqrt{r_3}-1)=0 \\Rightarrow r_3=1, \\frac19.\\]\" width=\"697\" height=\"37\" />\nSumming these two yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/7/c07c3d642b8bb259259a5b3016e65a8b2a586b8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\frac{10}{9}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"40\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/b/0fb67c7e7f09ba88987f834d2b21cce1592f3efa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] // By Elephant200 size(8cm); draw(circle((0,1),1), linewidth(1.2)); draw((-1,0)--(3,0), linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((1,1/4),1/4), linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((2/3,1/9),1/9), red+linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((2,1),1), red+linewidth(1.2)); [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"192\" />\n~Elephant200\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Clever_Manipulation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Clever_Manipulation)\">Solution 2 (Clever Manipulation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Clever Manipulation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In general, let the left and right outer circles and the center circle have radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/b/a7b236356b58f659ef3b68fe9bdb6e48399210fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1,r_2,r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"13\" />. When three circles are tangent as described in the problem, we can deduce <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4ff3a53b3934ea3db32cf76a95a5da542c5bdb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{r_3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}{\\sqrt{r_1}+\\sqrt{r_2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"145\" height=\"46\" /> by Pythagorean theorem. \n</p><p>Setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/2991165b0f5d50f7fda06f7262eb3062af4cc1ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1=1, r_2=\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"38\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/1/171a0261e694b674979693851573f75b88278d7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_3=\\frac19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" />, and setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/0/a801efb5a35b1b64d06aaf021007001fc624be66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1=1,r_3=\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"38\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/b/80b4fb53ec1a2e8a597b9a9beb4d3055b96ae6f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_2=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" />. Thus our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/f/fffc7368e8e7b77c88b0b4bf732c88c60e27b439.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"42\" height=\"30\" />. \n</p><p>~Mintylemon66\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Descartes.E2.80.99s_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Descartes’s_Theorem)\">Solution 3 (Descartes’s Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Descartes’s Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Descartes’s Theorem states that for curvatures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/9/959171e83184f30b21d6e5ed80ae54b57f7fa4f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_1,k_2,k_3,k_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" /> we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e9851661e4ae4dc17ab44075dd85d2d22e78fbe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k_1+k_2+k_3+k_4)^2=2(k_1^2+k_2^2+k_3^2+k_4^2)\\]\" width=\"337\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>with a curvature being the reciprocal of the radius of a circle, being positive if it is externally tangent to other circles, negative if it is internally tangent to other circles, and zero if we consider a line as a degenerate circle. Here our curvatures are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/4/2e4e2847289adc48a072ea004e7b1c1bf866fbe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_1=1,k_2=4,k_3=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"169\" height=\"18\" />, and we wish to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da4ccd7a2fe7fb7e9ba88a8a75c7a37f0b9867e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />. Plugging these values into our formula yields:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60a58028fb5f5096d7f980b24c2c99c49fd89e7a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1+4+0+k_4)^2=2(1^2+4^2+0^2+k_4^2)\\]\" width=\"310\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/3/d534360a2223385a4008ac4eb1fc62823236baf6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(5+k_4)^2=2(17+k_4^2)\\]\" width=\"172\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/d/f6dcd1e7999d324cc2d4e2786be413badc7fc2ee.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_4^2+10k_4+25=2k_4^2+34\\]\" width=\"206\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/7/d472275707a6b1d102e21f1aed67e066b6734973.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_4^2-10k_4+9=0\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/5/1a51828fcd8fb737c7f9d19919a73ef35d7dee21.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_4=1,9\\]\" width=\"66\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>The curvature and the radius are reciprocals, so our radii must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, and their sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e756ca7755976ac9f85057f43861342b093041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{10}{9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~eevee9406\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Advanced_Guesswork.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Advanced_Guesswork)\">Solution 4 (Advanced Guesswork)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Advanced Guesswork)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Looking at the diagram, we see that there must be a circle with a radius smaller than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/d/4cd0f9f9869b39e79527f9010293adf7bfe5210f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, and there must be a circle with a radius close to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Looking at the answers, we can assume that the answer choice is above <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, eliminating the first two answers. We can also assume that some students will only solve for the smaller circle, while others may only solve for the larger circle. It would be reasonably safe to say that one of the answers must be the radius of the smaller circle, while another must be the radius of the bigger circle. Looking at the answers, we see that  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/0/bd03925ad4b07379bac4f9546a313a689624633a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are reasonably close to the radii of the missing circles in the diagram, so we add them up to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e756ca7755976ac9f85057f43861342b093041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{10}{9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~YTH\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Essentially_Solution_3.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Essentially_Solution_3)\">Solution 5 (Essentially Solution 3)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Essentially Solution 3)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For a problem like this with three externally tangent circles that are all tangent to a line, we can use a specific form of\nDescartes's Circle Theorem. Let the curvatures of the three circles be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed2b055c89a98960f69ada4b7990044d435b27b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1, r_2, r_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"11\" />. The curvature of a circle is just\nthe reciprocal of the radius. Then we get, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ac0842d5fea0cf64ac96771eed7f44423b44f4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(r_1 + r_2 + r_3)^2 = 2(r_1^2 + r_2^2 + r_3^2)\\]\" width=\"250\" height=\"20\" /> Plugging in the information we already have, we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/7/d770f1c61966560ce704fadb303d8cfb51a15a23.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1 + 4 + r_3)^2 = 2(1^2 + 4^2 + r_3^2)\\]\" width=\"238\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/b/83b0de0733892ca62427f71d13c2e3cce9aec8f2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(5 + r_3)^2 = 2(17 + r_3^2)\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/f/1bfd3469edf970e4a48194887cc2a3c7eca57252.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[25 + 10r_3 + r_3^2 = 34 + 2r_3^2\\]\" width=\"200\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89f359c8a127a53179949084ae3c356e47bc5c6c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_3^2 - 10r_3 - 9 = 0\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/609cdec77750599848d3463a63563087d1d6cf6e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(r_3 - 1)(r_3 - 9) = 0\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"18\" />\nSo, we get our two curvatures: 1 and 9. The radii equivalent are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Adding these up, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e756ca7755976ac9f85057f43861342b093041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{10}{9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~Mr.Lightning\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Metasolving.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Metasolving)\">Solution 6 (Metasolving)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Metasolving)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By looking at the answer choices, we realize that the answers are all sums of each other, and thus think \"The AMC problem committee made the answers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> so the people who only got one of the answers will pick either of the first two answers and those who counted one of the answers twice would pick either of the last two answers\", and realize that the answers are most likely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Confirming with a ruler, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> + <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e756ca7755976ac9f85057f43861342b093041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{10}{9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />, which is the solution. \n</p><p>∼Glowworm\n</p><p>Minor issue with this is that 10/9 is also an answer choice, and 1/9 + 10/9 = 11/9 which is also an answer choice, so it comes down to a bit of luck.\n</p><p>~meikh_neiht\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7.28practically_similar_to_the_other_solutions.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7(practically_similar_to_the_other_solutions)\">Solution 7(practically similar to the other solutions)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7(practically similar to the other solutions)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can immediately see that we can put a circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> outside of the two circles already drawn that are tangent to the circles. If you brought a ruler with you, you can confirm this. We now find the radius of the circle between the circles of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />.. By drawing out the diagram, a geometry hack is to draw lines connecting the centers of the circles. Using the fact that tangent lines to the radius are perpendicular, we can construct a trapezoid with heights of lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, a slant base of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/c/a3c6858109db2726d39b81fc9df7a2bb67664974.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" />. To find the base of the trapezoid, we can construct a right triangle with the slant base as the hypotenuse and a leg of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/d/bfd498bb281267a29997d213e3ab55d0ee7c37fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" />. Therefore, we see we have a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/1/af1a2cb4ff1720f169ddde1726c1a5a1ab816ae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3-4-5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> right triangle and the length of the base of the trapezoid is conveniently just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Let the radius of the circle between the two given circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. By drawing the center of this circle and connecting the radius to the radii of the other two circles, we can construct two more trapezoids similar to the initially big one we constructed: one with perpendicular heights of lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and a slant base of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/7/dd72640b8ae1eed137147f448226138a3180327b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, and another with perpendicular heights of lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and a slant base of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/5897895bda725e5235d47921f0c685d7f06e8d21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r + \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" />. To find the bases of these trapezoids,(which we know sums to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as we found earlier), we can use a similar trick by constructing right triangles. We can construct two right triangles: one with hypotenuse <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/7/dd72640b8ae1eed137147f448226138a3180327b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> and a leg of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/3/4935490ee5036cb7e50ab30858b6d02f87a4698d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 - r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, and another with hypotenuse <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/5897895bda725e5235d47921f0c685d7f06e8d21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r + \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" /> and a leg of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/5/6b52823043fb9db04132a4e769a77a3fd77476db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4} - r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" />. We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the other legs of these right triangles and we can set the sum to equal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Conveniently, the leg lengths turns out of be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/0/a6036f7def9dafc501b83c7c27f73597883053d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/3/6a3e9006ecc9debb184b411a0fb4e94a291b9b62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />, respectively, and therefore the sum will just be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c782a818e20ff834cf1a15bbbf0bc7a1bd24e17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{r}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"21\" />. Since this is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we can solve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/5/df54b7954e1fcc16f5b7ee0f83031c540622db95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{r} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"21\" /> to get the radius to be equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Now, we add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/4/594c8b267069448b58fe77ad60497eb30f763a4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/1/6717255cb76cb21041a9c983b8ebd7b8fe68728b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{10}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> and our answer is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e756ca7755976ac9f85057f43861342b093041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{10}{9}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"70\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/b/0fb67c7e7f09ba88987f834d2b21cce1592f3efa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] // By Elephant200 size(8cm); draw(circle((0,1),1), linewidth(1.2)); draw((-1,0)--(3,0), linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((1,1/4),1/4), linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((2/3,1/9),1/9), red+linewidth(1.2)); draw(circle((2,1),1), red+linewidth(1.2)); [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"192\" />\n</p><p>credit to Elephant200 for the diagram\n</p><p>~ilikemath247365\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_.28In_Less_Than_5_Mins_.E2.9A.A1.F0.9F.9A.80.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(In_Less_Than_5_Mins_⚡🚀)\">Video Solution 1 by Pi A